      ":question/id": "b82e933f-4398-5d40-9872-fca591718d5a",
      ":question/problem": "![](generate/scratch/17592186110755/assets/8cf6d93dbe33956aa6e1839a2c23db407c651b452359a8ee5877060c47e4475f.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{9},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta .$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "![](generate/scratch/17592186110755/assets/6a8aee33bd7da02b173ad0029b84bb3f8a1b3acb35a611c65d737d7299095af4.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=sin\u2061\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{9}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin\u2061\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin\u2061\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{9}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{9})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{39}{81}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{78}{81} \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta =\\frac{28}{27}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
